= Images used with permission

The portraits of Willie Whitelaw, Sir Keith Joseph, Jim Prior, Gordon Reece, Nigel Lawson, Sir Ian Gilmour, Cecil Parkinson, John Biffen and Norman St John-Stevas, and the photographs on the Commons cards, the Budget, Public Spending, Law and Order, Northern Ireland and Public Sector Pay cards and several parliamentary events, are used with permission. The Public Sector Pay photograph is by Peter Brooker / Rex Features, and the photographs for the second Winter of Discontent event, the unemployment milestones, Toxteth and the Ford strike are from Reportdigital. None of them is covered by the game's licence, and they may not be reused without permission.

The torch logo on the Party Affairs cards belongs to the Conservative Party, and the "Labour Isn't Working" poster is by Saatchi & Saatchi for the Conservative Party (1978). Neither is covered by the game's licence.

The pictures for the Winter of Discontent, the Iranian Embassy siege, the steel strike, the hunger strike, the Sun's endorsement, World in Action, Crosby and the devolution referendums, and the Campaign Trail and TV Debate cards, were supplied for the game. Their rights remain with their owners, and they may not be reused without permission.

= Music

All recordings come from Wikimedia Commons, converted to MP3, and are used under the licences given. The compositions are in the public domain.

Elgar, *Pomp and Circumstance* March No. 1. Performed by United States Marine Band ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AELGAR_Pomp_and_Circumstance_in_D%2C_Opus_39%2C_No._1_-_United_States_Marine_Band.mp3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Public domain.

Elgar, *Pomp and Circumstance* March No. 4. Performed by United States Army Band ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3APomp_and_Circumstance%2C_Op._39_-_No._4_-_United_States_Army_Band.mp3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Public domain.

Arne, "Rule, Britannia!". Performed by United States Army Strings ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A%27Rule%2C_Britannia%21%27_%28United_States_Army_Strings%29.oga" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Public domain.

Elgar, *Enigma Variations*: "Nimrod". Performed by Hallé Orchestra, conducted by John Barbirolli (1947) ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AElgar%3B_Enigma_variations%2C_Theme_IX._Nimrod.ogg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Public domain.

Holst, *The Planets*: "Jupiter, the Bringer of Jollity". Recording from Musopen ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AGustav_Holst_-_the_planets%2C_op._32_-_iv._jupiter%2C_the_bringer_of_jollity.ogg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Free use with attribution (Musopen).

Elgar, *The Crown of India*: "March of the Mogul Emperors". Performed by United States Marine Band ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Crown_of_India_Suite%2C_5._March_of_the_Mogul_Emperors_-_U.S._Marine_Band.ogg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Public domain.

Elgar, "Land of Hope and Glory". Performed by Clara Butt (1911) ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AClara_Butt_-_Land_Of_Hope_And_Glory.ogg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Public domain.

"God Save the Queen". Performed by United States Navy Band ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AUnited_States_Navy_Band_-_God_Save_the_Queen.oga" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Public domain.

Elgar, *Pomp and Circumstance* March No. 2. Performed by United States Army Band ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3APomp_and_Circumstance%2C_Op._39_-_No._2_-_United_States_Army_Band.mp3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Public domain.

Holst, *The Planets*: "Mars, the Bringer of War". Performed by United States Air Force Band ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHolst-_mars.ogg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Public domain.

Holst, *The Planets*: "Saturn, the Bringer of Old Age". Performed by United States Marine Band ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHOLST_%22The_Planets%2C_Op._32_5._Saturn%2C_the_Bringer_of_Old_Age%22_-_%22The_President%27s_Own%22_U.S._Marine_Band.opus" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Public domain.

Holst, "I Vow to Thee, My Country". Performed by Richard MS Irwin (Hymns Without Words) ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHWW_I_Vow_to_Thee.ogg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). GFDL, CC BY-SA 4.0.

"The Last Post". Performed by Sgt. Codie Lynn Williams, U.S. Marine Corps ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ALast_Post_bugle_call.ogg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Public domain.

"Danny Boy" (the Londonderry Air). Performed by Ernestine Schumann-Heink (1917) ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchumann-Heink_-_Danny_Boy_%28Londonderry_air%29_%281917%29.ogg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). Public domain.

Handel, "Zadok the Priest". Performed by St Matthew's Concert Choir, directed by Damien Giromella ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHandel_-_Zadok_the_Priest%2C_HWV_258_%28St_Matthew%27s_Concert_Choir%2C_Giromella%29.oga" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). CC BY 3.0 (the recording; the composition is in the public domain).

Purcell, March from *Music for the Funeral of Queen Mary*. Performed by Erinaceus (Wikimedia Commons) ({!<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AH._Purcell_-_March_from_Music_for_the_funeral_of_Queen_Mary.oga" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>!}). CC BY-SA 3.0, GFDL.

